{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"TRIAGE EVIDENCE 2026-08-11 (pre-implementation, no code changed): the enrichment-drain attribution in this ticket is incorrect, and BOTH limbs are the same defect in src/rebar/metrics/blame.py.\n\n1) The enrichment drain has NO prose scanner. Traced the full post-write subtree: _store/event_append.py:606 write_and_push -> _maybe_enrich_drain -> llm/enrich_drain.py:216 maybe_drain -> drain (:117) -> llm/enrich.py enrich -> llm/overlap/{queue,digest_sidecar}. Every ticket id in that subtree comes from os.listdir(tracker), i.e. an already-canonical directory name, which short-circuits on the fast path at _ids.py:239-241 and never reaches the >=4-char prefix branch (_ids.py:291-312) that prints 'Ambiguous prefix'. enrich.py:27-44 reads title/description/comments ONLY to assemble the LLM prompt; it never regex-scans that prose for ticket refs. Neither extract_ticket_refs nor a free-text resolve exists anywhere in llm/enrich.py, llm/enrich_drain.py, or llm/overlap/*. retrieve/judge/graph/wire are not on the drain path (they are reached from plan-review via wire.overlap_findings). Additionally, in the default async mode the drain child's stderr is redirected to .rebar/logs/enrich-drain.log (enrich_drain.py:193,209), so it could not have produced terminal noise even hypothetically.\n\n2) The actual emitter is metrics/blame.py:55 (_resolves_to), reached only from _commands/transition_close.py:246-248 _apply_caused_by (gated on target_status=closed AND ticket_type=bug), which runs AFTER the close commits. Candidates come from verify_commit.extract_ticket_refs, whose second source is the leading '<token>:' of the commit subject (verify_commit.py:78-81); a bare 4-hex subject prefix passes _is_safe and falls through to the ambiguous-prefix print. Unlike close_precheck.py:119, blame passes neither quiet=True nor a resolution cache. This matches the second reproduction comment exactly (b2bb, 28f9, a9dd, dbb2, 3e8b during two bug closes).\n\n3) The post-write HANG limb is now PROVEN, same file, same call: _find_fixing_commit (blame.py:60-72) runs 'git log --format=%H%x1f%B%x1e' over the ENTIRE branch history and calls _resolves_to per commit. _resolves_to recomputes resolve_ticket_id(bug_id, tracker) on EVERY commit, and any non-full/short/Jira id (an alias, or a bare 4-hex fragment) falls through to _scan_alias (_ids.py:170-217), which iterates every ticket directory and opens up to two JSON files per ticket. Nothing is memoized. Measured on this checkout: 3823 ticket directories, 2137 commits, 620 of which carry a bare '<4hex>:' subject; one unresolvable 4-hex candidate costs ~0.26s. The per-commit bug_id re-resolution alone is ~2137 x 0.26s ~= 9 minutes of pure alias scanning after the close has already committed, plus ~620 x 0.26s for the subject candidates. That is the 'lingering process', not a deadlock: an unbounded O(commits x tickets) scan in optional caused-by derivation.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR SCOPE: the decided fix ('the enrichment-drain prose scanner must stop treating bare 4-hex fragments as prefixes; commit-subject resolution keeps today's behavior') has no implementable target -- the drain scanner does not exist, and the only defective path is precisely the commit-subject resolution that the decision excludes. Awaiting an operator call on which limb to change. The docs sweep (stop advertising 4-digit references in AGENTS.md and docs/*) is unaffected and remains valid as specified."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786503550632830001,"uuid":"98871968-0687-4975-9107-392683b6b8e2"}